    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The impact of vertebrate herbivores on plant communities varies depending on environmental conditions and the type of damage, which can be consumptive or nonconsumptive. While medium-to-large herbivores are known to significantly impact plant populations through trampling and other nonconsumptive interactions, it remains unclear how human disturbance influences this impact. Previous work suggests that disturbance can cause vertebrates to shift their habitat usage to less disturbed areas. Accordingly, I predicted that vertebrate herbivores would have a stronger impact on plant populations in areas removed from human disturbance. Furthermore, most previous studies have not distinguished between consumptive and nonconsumptive effects when examining vertebrate herbivore impact. Using an experimental approach, I examined how the impact of deer on plant communities through nonconsumptive interactions may vary under contrasting levels of human disturbance. The impact of deer and other vertebrates was measured using artificial seedling models in Maryland and Washington D.C. woodland areas with contrasting disturbance defined by the proximity to human walking trails. My results indicate that deer have a greater nonconsumptive impact on understory plants in less disturbed habitats and suggest that the reduction of nonconsumptive herbivory in disturbed areas may affect plant communities’ regeneration patterns. These findings shed light on how herbivores interact with their environment in heterogeneously disturbed urban landscapes. Understanding variable deer impact on plants through trampling has implications for management of natural and human-dominated landscapes, with regulation of human disturbance having the potential to be used as a plant-protection strategy. This research contributes to our understanding of how human activity may influence the structure of herbivore-plant interactions by demonstrating that anthropogenic disturbance decreases the effects of nonconsumptive herbivory on plant communities. Further research is warranted to determine how this may translate into community regeneration patterns along disturbance gradients.",
